Welcome back. Topic eight. Classes, inheritance, and pattern matching.

This is the most divergent topic in the entire curriculum. Object-oriented mental models split most sharply across these six languages. Single versus multiple inheritance. Primary constructors versus declaration syntax. Interfaces versus traits versus abstract classes. Records versus case classes versus data classes. And a five-rung pattern-matching ladder we have hinted at since topic four.

Six clusters today. Class declaration and constructors. Inheritance and abstract and sealed. Interfaces, traits, and mixins. Data-shaped classes — the records, case classes, data classes, and dataclasses. Singletons and companions. And finally pattern matching with destructuring.

Start with class declaration and constructors.

Where the constructor body lives, and how fields get initialized, are the loudest divergences here. Java requires a separate constructor body. Scala and Kotlin have a primary constructor in the class header. Python uses underscore underscore init. JavaScript class syntax, since ES six, is sugar over prototype chain assignment.

Java's constructor is a method-shaped block with no return type, named the same as the class. You assign each field by hand inside. Five-line ceremony for a two-field point class.

Scala writes class Point, parens, val x colon Int comma val y colon Int. The class header doubles as the constructor signature. The val keyword on each parameter declares a read-only field of that name, initialized from the constructor argument. Two read-only fields and the constructor that initializes them, in one line. Coming from Java, this is the largest single ergonomic win in the language.

Kotlin matches Scala's primary constructor syntax. Class Point, parens, val x colon Int comma val y colon Int. Same idea, same one-liner.

TypeScript adds parameter properties for the same effect. Constructor, parens, public x colon number comma private y colon number. The public or private modifier on a constructor parameter declares both the field and the assignment. This is the only place TypeScript's class syntax diverges from JavaScript; the runtime is plain JavaScript with explicit assignments compiled in.

Python writes def underscore underscore init, parens, self comma x comma y, colon, then self dot x equals x, self dot y equals y. The double-assignment ceremony is similar to Java's. Python three point seven added the at dataclass decorator to eliminate it for plain data classes — we will get to that in section four.

JavaScript writes constructor parens x comma y parens, then this dot x equals x. Class fields, since ES twenty twenty two, let you declare fields in the class body — x semicolon — and initialize them inline with x equals zero.

Field declaration. Five of the six declare fields with a type annotation in the class body. Java public final int x. TypeScript x colon number. Kotlin val x colon Int. Python is dynamically typed — fields appear when you assign to them, no declaration needed. Type hints, x colon int, are advisory and checked by mypy.

Read-only and mutable. Java uses final for read-only and the absence of final for mutable. Scala and Kotlin use val for read-only and var for mutable. TypeScript uses readonly. Python has no enforcement — convention is to prefix with a single underscore for "this is internal", or to use the property decorator for true immutability.

Getters and setters. Java requires manual get-x and set-x methods — endless ceremony, mostly auto-generated by IDE shortcuts or Lombok. Scala and Kotlin generate them automatically from val and var declarations. JavaScript and TypeScript have the get and set keywords inside class bodies. Python uses the at-property decorator to turn a method into an attribute-access target.

Move to inheritance, abstract, and sealed.

Single versus multiple inheritance. Five of the six are single-inheritance. A class can extend exactly one base class. Multiple inheritance is achieved through interfaces or traits.

Python is the lone exception. Class Sub, parens, A comma B, colon — a Python class can have multiple base classes. Method resolution order uses the C three linearization algorithm. Predictable, but not always intuitive. Most Python codebases avoid deep multiple-inheritance hierarchies. Mixins — small classes that add a method or two — are the common pattern.

The extends keyword. Java, Scala, JavaScript, TypeScript all use the literal word extends. Kotlin uses a colon — class Sub colon Super. Python uses parens around the parent class name in the class declaration.

Calling the super constructor. Java requires super, parens, args, semicolon, as the first statement of the constructor body. Scala and Kotlin invoke the super constructor in the class header — class Sub, parens, x, parens, extends Super, parens, x. JavaScript and TypeScript use super, parens, args, inside the constructor body. Python uses super, parens, parens, dot underscore underscore init, parens, args.

Now the most opinionated choice on this whole table. Kotlin's *closed by default*.

In Kotlin, every class is final unless you declare it open. Every method is final unless declared open. Inspired by Effective Java's "design and document for inheritance, or else prohibit it". Coming from Java — where everything is open by default — this catches Kotlin newcomers constantly. You write a class intending to subclass it later, then can't, because the parent class is not open. The fix is one keyword. The cultural friction is real.

Abstract classes and methods. Universal across five of six. Java, Scala, Kotlin, TypeScript, and Python all have abstract class and abstract method declarations. JavaScript has nothing native. TypeScript adds abstract class and abstract method as a compile-time check. At runtime it is a regular class. Calling new AbstractFoo is a TypeScript error but a JavaScript success.

Python uses the abc module — class Foo, parens, ABC, colon — and the at-abstractmethod decorator. Calling Foo with no override of the abstract method raises TypeError at instantiation time.

Sealed hierarchies. The "this trait or class can only have these subclasses" mechanism. Scala has sealed trait. Kotlin has sealed class. Java added sealed in version seventeen, with a permits clause naming the allowed subclasses. The benefit is exhaustiveness checking inside pattern matching — if you match on a sealed type and miss a case, the compiler tells you.

JavaScript and TypeScript do not have native sealed. TypeScript's discriminated unions get close but rely on a tag field and assertion functions for exhaustiveness. Python does not have sealed — you encode the same idea with enums or unions of types in type hints.

Override keyword. Scala and Kotlin require override. The compiler enforces it — overriding a parent method without the keyword is an error. Java has the at-Override annotation, which is *optional* but strongly recommended — IDEs warn if it is missing. JavaScript and TypeScript imply override by name match. TypeScript four point three added an explicit override modifier as an opt-in. Python three point twelve added the at-override decorator as a hint, also optional.

Move to interfaces, traits, and mixins.

How you express "implements this contract" without single-base inheritance. Each of these languages handles it differently.

Java has interface. A contract with abstract method signatures. Since version eight, interfaces can have default methods with implementation, partially closing the gap with traits. Interfaces can declare static fields but not instance state.

Scala has trait. The most flexible mixin mechanism on this list. Traits can have method bodies, instance state, and constructor parameters in Scala three. A class can extend one trait or many — class Foo extends A with B with C — and Scala linearizes the supertype hierarchy into a single chain.

Trait linearization is the diamond problem solved by ordering. When multiple traits define the same method, the linearization rule picks the rightmost-trait's version. Calling super dot m walks down the chain, not just to the immediate parent. The order matters. Extends A with B differs from extends B with A. The mental model is *stackable modifications*. Powerful, but easy to get tangled.

Kotlin has interface with default method bodies. A class can implement multiple interfaces. When two interfaces define the same method, Kotlin requires you to disambiguate explicitly with super angle T angle dot m parens parens — naming which parent's version you want.

JavaScript has nothing at the language level. The folklore mixin pattern is a function that takes a class and returns an extended class — class composition by function application. No syntax, but commonly used.

TypeScript has interface — but it is *structural*, not nominal. Two interfaces with identical members are interchangeable, regardless of name. A function expecting "object with a name string" accepts any object with a name string field. You never have to write implements Foo, though you can. Declaration merging lets multiple interface declarations with the same name combine into one.

Python has two mechanisms. Class Foo, parens, ABC, colon — the abstract base class — is nominal. You inherit from it explicitly. Class Foo, parens, Protocol, colon — added in PEP five forty four, version three point eight — is structural. Anything with the right methods satisfies the protocol, regardless of inheritance. Both coexist. Type-checking tools like mypy support both.

Move to data-shaped classes.

Each language has a sugared form for "plain data with auto-generated equality, hashing, and printing". Each is named differently and each has slightly different rules.

Java has record, since version sixteen. Record Point, parens, double x comma double y, parens — declares an immutable data class. The compiler synthesizes the constructor, accessors named after the components, equals, hashCode, and toString. Records are implicitly final and implicitly extend java dot lang dot Record. They cannot extend other classes but can implement interfaces. Java twenty one added record patterns for destructuring inside switch and instanceof.

Scala has case class. Case class Point, parens, x colon Int comma y colon Int. The original on the JVM and the most powerful. Auto equality, hashing, toString, copy, an unapply extractor for pattern matching, and JSON serialization via libraries — all from one declaration. Sealed trait plus a set of case classes is the canonical algebraic data type encoding.

Kotlin has data class. Data class Point, parens, val x colon Int comma val y colon Int. Sits between Scala and Java. Allows var fields, unlike Java records. Has copy parens with named-argument modification — point dot copy, parens, x equals ten — for non-destructive update. Has componentN methods for positional destructuring — val parens x comma y parens equals point. Cannot inherit from another data class but can extend a regular class or implement interfaces.

Python has at dataclass, since version three point seven. A decorator on a class definition that synthesizes underscore underscore init, underscore underscore repr, underscore underscore eq, and with frozen equals True, underscore underscore hash. More flexible than the JVM equivalents — you can add custom methods, inherit from other dataclasses, mix in defaults — and the auto-generated code is plain Python you can read in dataclasses dot py.

JavaScript and TypeScript have no built-in equivalent. The plain object literal — curly x colon one comma y colon two curly — is the canonical data shape, but equality is reference, hashCode does not exist, and there is no built-in copy syntax. The spread idiom — curly dot dot dot foo comma x colon ten curly — gives you a copy with one field changed. For value-equality, use a library or stringify-and-compare.

Copy with modification — the *non-destructive update* pattern. Scala's foo dot copy, parens, x equals ten. Kotlin's foo dot copy, parens, x equals ten. JavaScript's curly dot dot dot foo comma x colon ten curly. Python's dataclasses dot replace, parens, foo comma x equals ten. Java's records have *no built-in copy* — you write a wither method by hand, or use Lombok's at Builder. This is one of the rare places Java records fall short of the others.

Move to singletons and companions.

How each language expresses "one instance, accessible by name" and "static-like state attached to a class".

Scala has the cleanest singleton on this list. Object Foo curly body curly. Declares a singleton named Foo. You write Foo dot bar parens parens to call methods. There is no separate static-versus-instance distinction — the singleton *is* the static side. A *companion object* is an object with the same name as a class, in the same file — it has access to private members of the class. The companion is where you put factory methods, constants, and apply functions for case-class style construction.

Kotlin has companion object — Scala's idea adapted to JVM interop. Inside a class body, you write companion object curly body curly, and members are accessed as Foo dot bar from Kotlin code. From Java, the same members are accessed as Foo dot Companion dot bar — because under the hood, the companion is a nested class. The at JvmStatic annotation on companion members exposes them as true Java statics.

Java uses the static keyword. Static methods and fields belong to the class, not the instance. The enum-of-one idiom — public enum Singleton with a single value INSTANCE — is a common pattern from Effective Java for thread-safe lazy singletons.

JavaScript modules make module-level const the natural singleton. Each module is loaded once. Its exports are shared. Class-level static, since ES six, gives static methods and fields on the constructor function — class Foo, curly, static x equals one, curly. Reference Foo dot x.

Python's three method decorators are unique to this list. Plain def m, parens, self, parens, is an instance method. At staticmethod makes it a function attached to the class — no self, no cls. At classmethod passes the class as the first argument — useful for alternate constructors. At classmethod, def from underscore string, parens, cls comma s, parens, return cls, parens, parsed, parens. The first-class status of class objects in Python makes alternate-constructor patterns much more natural than the JVM languages. A subclass that calls from underscore string returns an instance of the subclass automatically — without overriding the method.

Move to pattern matching and destructuring. The last cluster.

The control-flow side of match was covered in topic four. This section focuses on *destructuring* — pulling fields out of a value while matching its shape — and *type patterns* — checking type and binding the narrowed value in one step.

Scala has the most powerful pattern matching of these six. Custom extractors via unapply. Sealed-trait exhaustiveness. Nested patterns. Guards. As-patterns — name at-sign pattern — to capture both the whole and the parts. A match expression on a sealed trait is the canonical encoding for "visit each case and the compiler will tell me if I miss one".

Java twenty one's pattern switch is the modern equivalent and intentionally Scala-inspired. Switch, parens, shape, parens, then case Circle, parens, double r, parens, arrow, Math dot PI times r times r. With sealed-class exhaustiveness. Records and sealed classes were added precisely to make this useful — without records there is nothing to destructure, without sealed classes there is no exhaustiveness check.

Python's match, since three point ten, is structural pattern matching. Case Point, parens, x equals zero, parens, matches a Point with x zero. Case bracket a comma star rest bracket destructures a sequence. Case curly quote name quote colon n curly matches a dict with a name key. The walrus operator — case f colon equals Foo, parens, parens — captures the whole value while matching its type. The dunder attribute underscore underscore match underscore args lets a class participate as an extractor — list the field names in the order positional matching should use them.

Kotlin's when is type-pattern only. There is no destructuring inside when. Kotlin uses componentN methods for positional destructuring elsewhere — val parens x comma y parens equals point. But inside when, you write is Foo and access fields manually after the smart-cast. Less expressive than Scala's, Python's, or Java twenty one's patterns. More than enough for most code.

JavaScript and TypeScript have no pattern matching. TC thirty nine has had a pattern-matching proposal in stage one for years — it has not landed. Destructuring assignment — const curly x comma y curly equals obj — is the closest substitute. Works on objects, arrays, and nested combinations. Combine with if-else-if chains and instanceof checks for the rest. TypeScript's discriminated unions and type guards are the closest the language gets to exhaustiveness.

A few cross-cutting notes before we close.

Pattern matching is a sliding scale. We covered this rung by rung in topic four. To recap. Java pre fourteen — constants only. Java fourteen — switch expression. Java sixteen — records. Java twenty one — type and record patterns plus guards. JavaScript and TypeScript — no pattern matching, only destructuring assignment. Kotlin — type and range patterns in when, no nested destructuring. Python three point ten — full structural matching with class extractors via dunder match args. Scala — full structural matching with custom extractors via unapply. Where each language sits on this ladder is worth more than memorizing the case syntax of any single one.

JavaScript class is sugar over prototypes. Pre ES six, you wrote function Foo, parens, parens, then this dot x equals one, then Foo dot prototype dot bar equals function. ES six class syntax is the same thing with a nicer surface. There is no separate class concept at runtime. A class is a function whose prototype property holds the methods. Instanceof walks the prototype chain. Understanding this distinction matters when you encounter older codebases or non-class objects with the same shape.

Composition over inheritance — universal advice, Kotlin enforces it. Every language's design guidance for the past twenty years has been "prefer composition over inheritance". Kotlin is the only one that bakes this into syntax. Classes and methods are final by default. You opt in with the open keyword. The fix is one keyword. The cultural friction is real.

To recap. Class declaration diverges most on whether the constructor lives in the header — Scala and Kotlin yes, everyone else no. Inheritance is single across five of six; Python is the lone multiple-inheritance language with C three method resolution order. Interfaces split into nominal — Java, Scala, Kotlin — and structural — TypeScript, Python Protocol. Data-shaped classes have a sugared form in five of six — Java record, Scala case class, Kotlin data class, Python at dataclass. JavaScript and TypeScript use plain object literals with no auto-generated equality. Singletons are cleanest in Scala via the object keyword. Pattern matching is a five-rung ladder. Scala and Java twenty one and Python sit at the top. JavaScript and TypeScript sit at the bottom with nothing native.

That is topic eight, and that completes the polyglot reference curriculum. Eight topics. Values. Names, types, and generics. Operators and expressions. Control flow. Functions. Collections and strings. Errors, null safety, and resources. And classes, inheritance, and pattern matching. Together they cover the universal cross-cutting features that exist in all six languages. The deeper language-specific idioms — Scala implicits and givens, Kotlin coroutines, Python decorators, JavaScript event loop semantics, Java JVM internals — live in the per-language repos. From here, pick whichever language you want to go deepest in, and the polyglot anchors will let you contrast it against the other five as you go.
